The easiest (and wrong!) way to save and load data between scenes is using
PlayerPrefs. Essentially, PlayerPrefs is a hash map that allows to store and
retrieve values at any time, and it is not destroyed or deleted while closing a
scene. PlayerPrefs gives access to the following methods:
float GetFloat(string key, float defVal = 0.0f) / SetFloat(string key, float val)
int GetInt(string key, int defVal = 0.0f) / SetInt(string key, int val)
string GetString(string key, string defVal = 0.0f) / SetString(string key, string val)
bool HasKey(string key), DeleteKey(string key), DeleteAll();
void Save(): Writes all modified preferences to disk (not recommended to call
during gameplay for performance reasons). By default Unity writes preferences
to disk on Application Quit.
1 public void LoadAddOnClick ( int levelIndex )
2 {
3 PlayerPrefs . SetInt ( " Health " , 100) ;
4 SceneManager . LoadScene ( levelIndex , LoadSceneMode . Additive ) ;
5 }
1 void OnLevelLoaded ( int levelIndex ) {
2 player . health = PlayerPrefs . GetInt ( " Health " ) ;
3 }
Check http://docs.unity3d.com/ScriptReference/PlayerPrefs.html for location of saved
files on each OS.
